{"episodes":{"161323":"During the afternoon of Thursday, September 16th, a cold front laid across west-central, and central Minnesota. Ahead of this cold front, temperatures soared into the upper 80s to lower 90s. A strong mid-level CAP held through most of the afternoon before a line of clouds started to develop along this front. Storms quickly develop once the CAP broke and with ample wind shear and instability, several of the storms produced large hail, with the largest size measured at 2.5 in diameter. These storms weakened by sunset as they moved slowly eastward toward east central Minnesota.","161450":"A line of thunderstorms produced strong winds over the upper Chesapeake Bay.","161451":"A thunderstorm produced gusty winds over the upper Chesapeake Bay.","161452":"A few thunderstorms became severe producing damaging wind gusts over western and north-central Maryland during the afternoon, as well as over northeastern Maryland during the evening.","161694":"As a surface trough passed north of the islands, it triggered heavy showers over portions of the state.  The downpours produced ponding on roadways, and small stream and drainage ditch flooding.  There were no reports of significant property damage or injuries.","162119":"Deep SW flow and tropical moisture on the east side of Nicholas brought waves of showers and thunderstorms from west to east through the day. An upper low was west of the region with waves of PVA rotating overhead which enhanced heavy rainfall.","162828":"Isolated thunderstorms developed over northeastern Colorado during the early evening hours of September 16th. Storms became severe as they crossed the border into Deuel county. Hail up to half dollar size and wind gusts up to 67 MPH were reported with the strongest storms.","163059":"A cold front marching across the Northland acted as the catalyst for the development of severe thunderstorms. Storms were initially delayed due to a lingering stratus deck but once convective initiation took hold of the region, the strong ingredients in place lead to numerous severe storms across northeastern Minnesota that produced damaging winds and hail as large as 3 inches in diameter.","163099":"A cold front moved across southern New England overnight, but stalled as it approached southeastern MA.  Thunderstorms caused scattered wind damage in southeastern MA between 7 and 745 AM EDT.","163278":"A light flow aloft along with an unstable atmosphere led to slow moving showers and thunderstorms. Heavy amounts of rain led to instances of flooding.","163279":"A light flow aloft along with an unstable atmosphere led to slow moving showers and thunderstorms. Heavy amounts of rain led to instances of flooding and flash flooding.","163374":"A light flow aloft along with an unstable atmosphere led to slow moving showers and thunderstorms. Heavy amounts of rain led to instances of flooding.","163446":"A dryline was setup through central New Mexico with a weak disturbance moving southeast through the region.  A few thunderstorms developed and a wind gust of 58 mph was reported near Holloman AFB.","163487":"Isolated strong to severe thunderstorms formed across the area late in the evening of September 15th, and persisted into the early morning hours of  September 16th.","163488":"Isolated strong to severe thunderstorms formed across the area late in the evening of September 15th, and persisted into the early morning hours of  September 16th.","163528":"Scattered thunderstorms along a frontal boundary produced heavy rain which caused flash flooding across portions of central Virginia.","163533":"Deep tropical moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Nicholas overspread the area and led to the development of bands of very heavy rainfall. The slow motion of these bands produced copious amounts of rain which led to flash flooding across portions of northwest Florida."},"events":[{"begin":"2021-09-16T00:00Z","county":"Honolulu","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0